---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../components/SiteHeader.astro";
---
<Layout title="Introduction — Bereshit / In Principio" prose>
  <div class="wrap">
    <SiteHeader active="/introduction/" />
    <h1 class="title">Introduction</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">What this edition is, how it was made, and what it found.</p>

    <div class="intro">
      <p>
        This is an edition of five verses and of what was said about them. Genesis 1:1–5 is read as
        ten cruxes — a crux being a difficulty that more than one tradition felt it had to answer —
        and on each crux every witness the edition could find is printed in its own language and in
        English, dated, placed, sourced to the column or the leaf it came from, and threaded to the
        other witnesses it cites, echoes, contests, transmits, or merely runs parallel to. The unit is
        not the commentary and not the commentator. It is the argument: one continuous piece of
        reasoning on one verse from one work. Augustine has five of them on the second verse alone.
      </p>
      <p>
        Three benches sit on each page. The Latin runs from Origen in Rufinus's translation to
        Nicholas of Lyra and Paul of Burgos, and is drawn almost entirely from the Patrologia — the
        exceptions, a fourth-century Platonist and a fifteenth-century printed Bible, are transcribed
        from page images and say so. The rabbinic runs from the targums through Bereshit Rabbah and
        the Bavli to Rashi, Ibn Ezra and Ramban, and is drawn from Sefaria. The third bench is the
        smallest and the oldest: the Septuagint, and Philo of Alexandria, who read these verses in
        Greek before either of the other benches existed and who turns out to be the head of
        arguments both of them make. A witness's English is either a published translation, named
        and licensed, or a fresh draft made for this edition against a fixed list of renderings and
        marked as a draft until it was read. The colophon accounts for every text by licence.
      </p>
      <p>
        The threads are the edition's claims, and each carries its evidence. A thread says
        <i>cites</i> only when a witness names its source; <i>echoes</i> when the reuse is verbatim
        and unattributed; <i>contests</i> when one reading argues with another it has actually met;
        <i>transmits</i> when a text is the channel by which a reading reached a later bench. A
        thread that says <i>parallel</i> is a confession: the same image, the same argument, the
        same proof text, on two benches, with no channel between them that the edition can show. There
        are more of those than of any other kind, and the edition has not tried to explain them away.
      </p>
      <p>
        What the ten findings add up to is not one thing but four. The first is that the two large
        benches, reading the same five verses in different languages for a thousand years, keep
        arriving at the same place by different roads. Both gloss the first word of Scripture as
        <i>in wisdom</i> and prove it from a wisdom text — a different wisdom text on each bench, and
        a different Wisdom. Both decide what the dividing of light from darkness means by reading a
        silence, and they find the same silence at consecutive clauses of the same verse. Both refuse
        the question of whether heaven or earth came first, and refuse it with the same psalm. Three
        benches choose the same two verses out of the Pentateuch to test the divine plural, by the
        same criterion, and none of them has read another. The pairing is not borrowed. It is what
        the Hebrew does to anyone who reads it carefully.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second is that where contact existed, it was lost, and it was lost in both directions.
        Chalcidius in the fourth century reports Origen persuaded of the non-temporal beginning
        <i>ab Hebraeis</i>, quoting the rabbinic bench's own verse, and nobody read Chalcidius.
        Basil's Latin Hexaemeron carries an accurate report of the rabbinic reading of
        <i>Faciamus hominem</i>, and Bede copies it at Jarrow keeping every sentence of the argument
        and removing every Jew in it. And the rabbinic bench, which cites Philo not once, lost the one
        Jewish reader of Genesis 1 whose reading of its first verse its own commentary most resembles;
        he survives because the church copied him, and was recovered for Judaism only in the
        sixteenth century. Jerome, the one Latin who read the Hebrew, refuted the reading of
        <i>in principio</i> as <i>in the Son</i>, and the Carolingians copied his page and dropped the
        refutation. Transmission on these benches is not a channel. It is a filter, and what it
        filters out is the knowledge that anyone else was in the room.
      </p>
      <p>
        The third is one page. At Genesis 1:4, in a printed Latin Bible of 1487, Nicholas of Lyra
        calls the answer of Rabanus and Peter Lombard a piece of trifling and takes the reading he
        wants from Rashi; Paul of Burgos, who had been a rabbi in that city until he was forty and
        was afterwards its bishop, replies that Lyra credits Rabbi Solomon the Hebrew with what our
        own doctors found first; and Matthias Doering, defending Lyra, restates the charge and
        changes one word — he attributes the <i>true</i> answer to an <i>unbeliever</i>. Nobody in
        that exchange denies that the answer is right. An early reader of this copy underlined
        <i>Ra. Sa.</i> in ink on both leaves. Every other page of the edition shows two traditions
        reading the same verses without knowing the other is there; this one shows what it sounds
        like when they do.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fourth is that the modern translations settle the cruxes by removing them. The ancient
        versions all kept <i>day one</i>; the English on both benches prints <i>the first day</i>,
        and the note that exists to ask why it is not <i>first</i> glosses the lemma as <i>first</i>.
        The JPS Tanakh prints <i>When God began to create</i>, which is Rashi's plain sense and Ibn
        Ezra's construct adopted as the text — the one reading no Latin witness could have raised,
        and the one Ramban had already refuted. The question each crux is made of survives only in
        the languages nobody now reads it in. That is the reason for printing the languages.
      </p>
      <p>
        A page is laid out as a daf. The verse sits in the centre in Hebrew, Latin and English, with
        the Septuagint and the Vulgate beneath it and the Glossa's marginal glosses beneath those.
        The Fathers are in the left column and Philo above them; the rabbinic bench is on the right;
        the Carolingian and twelfth-century schools run across the foot. Every note opens on its
        lemma and an excerpt; hovering opens the whole note and draws its threads across the page;
        clicking pins it. The dialogue pages show the same witnesses by verse rather than by crux,
        and the finding on each crux page is the editor's own reading of what the page shows.
      </p>
      <p class="sig">
        The edition was made in the late summer of 2026. Its data, its build, and every decision
        taken in making it are in the open repository, including the decisions that were taken and
        then reversed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .intro p{font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.6;max-width:68ch;margin:0 0 18px}
  .intro .sig{color:var(--mute);font-size:14.5px;margin-top:30px}
</style>
